Project Mu: the browser is mu.exe

Mu ships its browser as mu.exe; build.py package renames chrome.exe when it
packs the build (the build itself still produces chrome.exe, which Chromium's
build files and archive lists expect). This patch teaches the code that finds
or checks the browser by file name to use Mu's name:

  * chrome::kBrowserProcessExecutableName/Path (Windows). Among others: the
    check that turns on the "Microsoft-signed DLLs only" mitigation for
    renderers only when the browser runs from its own executable, Task
    Manager's CPU and memory sampling, and the notification helper that wakes
    the browser from a Windows notification.
  * installer::kChromeExe: start at login, protocol launches and the
    uninstall shortcut cleanup.
  * chrome_proxy.exe, which installed web apps' shortcuts start the browser
    through.

diff --git a/chrome/chrome_proxy/chrome_proxy_main_win.cc b/chrome/chrome_proxy/chrome_proxy_main_win.cc
index 776f544..d86a531 100644
--- a/chrome/chrome_proxy/chrome_proxy_main_win.cc
+++ b/chrome/chrome_proxy/chrome_proxy_main_win.cc
@@ -14,8 +14,10 @@
 
 namespace {
 
+// Project Mu: the browser ships as mu.exe (build.py package renames
+// chrome.exe), so code that finds or checks the browser by name uses Mu's.
 constexpr base::FilePath::CharType kChromeExecutable[] =
-    FILE_PATH_LITERAL("chrome.exe");
+    FILE_PATH_LITERAL("mu.exe");
 
 constexpr base::FilePath::CharType kChromeProxyExecutable[] =
     FILE_PATH_LITERAL("chrome_proxy.exe");
